// Chartolj! — English lesson translations, module 0 (Day Trading Basics).
// Translated in parts; each part adds lessons with Object.assign. Keys are LESSONS_DATA
window.LESSONS_EN = Object.assign(window.LESSONS_EN || {}, {
"0": {"t":"What Is Day Trading?","s":"The core philosophy of intraday trading","cards":[{"t":"Definition of day trading","b":"<p><strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Day trading</strong> means opening and closing a position within the same trading day — you never hold a position overnight. The goal is to profit from intraday price moves, not from long-term trends.</p>"},{"t":"How is it different from investing?","b":"<p>An investor holds an asset for weeks, months or years, based on the fundamental value of companies/assets. A day trader, by contrast, thinks in <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">minutes or hours</strong> and focuses almost entirely on the chart and price behaviour, not on long-term fundamentals.</p>"},{"t":"Why does a day trader close everything by the end of the day?","b":"<p>A position left open overnight carries <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">gap risk</strong> — because of news released after the close (e.g. company news, macro data), the market can open in the morning somewhere very different from where it closed the day before, and this can work against you before you can do anything.</p>"},{"t":"Who is it not recommended for?","b":"<p>Day trading is <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">not passive income</strong> — it requires active daily attention, fast decision-making and strict discipline. If someone can only look at the market occasionally, longer-term investing is a more realistic path for them.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What is the main characteristic of day trading?","o":["Opening and closing positions within the same trading day","Holding only dividend-paying stocks for the long term","Trading the market only once a week","Holding long-term positions continuously for years"],"e":"The essence of day trading is that every position is opened and closed on the same day, never held overnight."},{"q":"Why does a day trader not hold positions overnight?","o":["Because the market completely stops overnight for every asset","To avoid the risk of news during the non-tradable overnight hours","Because the law forbids overnight positions everywhere","Because the broker automatically closes every position at midnight"],"e":"This way the day trader avoids the risk of news arriving during the overnight, non-tradable hours."},{"q":"How is it different from swing trading?","o":["Day trading always holds positions longer than swing trading","There is no real difference, the two approaches are practically the same","Swing trading holds positions for days to weeks, day trading does not","Swing trading never holds a position for more than a single day"],"e":"Swing trading holds positions from days to weeks, while day trading always closes within the day."},{"q":"Which skill is especially important in day trading?","o":["In-depth knowledge of long-term fundamental analysis","Fast decision-making and disciplined risk management","Detailed, careful reading of a company's annual reports","Precise, up-to-date knowledge of dividend calendars"],"e":"Fast decision-making and disciplined risk management are especially important in day trading."},{"q":"Why is it riskier for many beginners than they think?","o":["It is not riskier, it is actually the safest form of trading","Because the broker always covers beginners' losses automatically","It demands fast, emotionally draining decisions and a lot of practice","Because day trading always brings guaranteed, instant profit"],"e":"Day trading demands fast, emotionally draining decisions that are hard to handle well without a lot of practice."}]},
"1": {"t":"Long and Short Positions","s":"How you can profit from both rising and falling prices","cards":[{"t":"What is a Long position?","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Long</strong> position means you buy the asset because you expect it to rise — you buy lower and want to sell higher. This is the traditional trading direction everyone knows.</p>"},{"t":"What is a Short position?","b":"<p>With a <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Short</strong> position you speculate on a falling price: in effect you \"borrow and sell\" the asset at the current price, then buy it back cheaper later — the difference is your profit. If the price rises instead of falling, you make a loss.</p>"},{"t":"Why does shorting matter in day trading?","b":"<p>For a day trader <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">it does not matter which way the market goes</strong> — all that matters is that there is movement. Because you can short, you can open positions in a falling market too; you do not have to wait for everything to rise.</p>"},{"t":"The risk of shorting: theoretically unlimited loss","b":"<p>With a Long position your maximum loss is the amount invested (the price cannot go below 0). With a Short position, however, <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">the price can theoretically rise without limit</strong> — that is why risk management is especially important for shorts; you should never open a short position without a stop-loss.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What is a Long position?","o":["Buying with the expectation that the price will rise","A mandatory waiting period before closing a position","The minimum holding period required by the broker","A bet on a falling price, profiting when it drops"],"e":"A Long position means buying the asset, expecting its price to rise."},{"q":"What is a Short position?","o":["Buying in the hope that the price will rise","Selling a borrowed asset and buying it back cheaper later","A type of long-term investment strategy held for years","A type of free demo account offered by the broker"],"e":"In a Short position we sell a borrowed asset, hoping to buy it back cheaper later."},{"q":"Why is the risk of a Short position theoretically unlimited?","o":["Because a Short position never carries real risk","After opening, the price can theoretically rise without limit","It is not unlimited, it is always limited to exactly the amount invested","Because the broker always limits the loss automatically"],"e":"Since the price can theoretically rise without limit, the loss on a Short position can also be unlimited."},{"q":"What do you need to open a Short position?","o":["Only a simple Market order, no other requirement","An asset borrowed from the broker or another market participant","Only a long-term, multi-year trading licence","The broker's written, personal approval every single time"],"e":"To open a Short position you have to borrow the asset, which you then sell on the market."},{"q":"When is a Long position profitable?","o":["When the price rises above the opening price","When the price falls below the opening price, causing a loss","Only if the position is held continuously for a year","Never, it always produces a loss for the trader"],"e":"A Long position is profitable when the price rises above the opening price."}]},
"2": {"t":"Leverage and Margin","s":"How to magnify your position — and your risk","cards":[{"t":"What is leverage?","b":"<p><strong style=\"color:#FF3D5A\">Leverage</strong> lets you open a position larger than your own capital — e.g. with 10x leverage, $1,000 of collateral controls a $10,000 position. It magnifies both profits and losses.</p>"},{"t":"What is margin?","b":"<p><strong style=\"color:#FF3D5A\">Margin</strong> is the collateral the broker/exchange requires to open and maintain a leveraged position. It is essentially your \"security deposit\" behind the position.</p>"},{"t":"What is a Margin Call?","b":"<p>If your position moves into loss and your collateral falls below the minimum level, the broker issues a <strong style=\"color:#FF3D5A\">Margin Call</strong> — either you deposit extra money, or the system automatically closes (liquidates) your position, often at the worst possible moment.</p>"},{"t":"Why is leverage the most common cause of beginner mistakes?","b":"<p>High leverage (e.g. 50x, 100x) means that <strong style=\"color:#FF3D5A\">a price move of just a few percent against you can wipe out your entire capital</strong>. As a beginner, using low (2–5x) or no leverage is much safer until you deeply understand risk management.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What is leverage?","o":["The broker's minimum registration balance","The broker's annual commission","Using borrowed capital to increase position size","The daily price change"],"e":"Leverage lets you open a position larger than your own using borrowed capital."},{"q":"What is margin?","o":["Your own capital that has to be put up as collateral","The daily opening price","The broker's profit","The official upper limit on the number of daily trades"],"e":"Margin is your own capital that has to be provided as collateral for a leveraged position."},{"q":"What is a margin call?","o":["A demand to deposit extra collateral because the position is losing","A kind of bonus","The official close of the trading day","A daily report from the broker"],"e":"A margin call is a demand to deposit extra collateral when losses push the margin level below the critical threshold."},{"q":"Why is high leverage risky?","o":["It always reduces risk","Even a small price move can cause a disproportionately large loss","It is only a theoretical concept","It is not risky, it is always safe"],"e":"With high leverage even a small price move can cause a loss that is disproportionately large compared with the capital invested."},{"q":"What happens if the margin level falls below the critical level and you do not deposit extra collateral?","o":["The broker cancels the debt","The position automatically doubles","Nothing, the position stays unchanged","The broker can automatically close (liquidate) the position"],"e":"If the margin level falls below the critical level and there is no extra collateral, the broker can automatically close (liquidate) the position."}]},
"3": {"t":"Order Types: Market, Limit, Stop","s":"How to place your order precisely","cards":[{"t":"Market order","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Market order</strong> is executed immediately at the best price currently available. Its advantage is guaranteed, instant execution; its drawback is that in a volatile market it can fill at a worse price than you saw on screen (slippage).</p>"},{"t":"Limit order","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Limit order</strong> is executed only at a price you specify or better. It gives you control over the price, but there is no guarantee it will fill at all if the market does not reach your level.</p>"},{"t":"Stop order (Stop-Loss)","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Stop order</strong> is triggered at a predefined price level and from then on executes as a market order — this is the most common way to limit losses: if the price moves against you to a given level, the stop closes the position automatically.</p>"},{"t":"Stop-Limit: a combination of the two","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Stop-Limit</strong> order becomes a limit order, not a market order, when the stop level is hit. This gives more precise price control, but the risk is that in a fast, strong move (gap) it may not fill at all, because the price jumps over the limit level.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What characterises a Market order?","o":["It never fills until the price reaches the specified level","It guarantees immediate execution at the current market price","It can only be placed before the official market open","It always fills only at a specified price or better"],"e":"A Market order guarantees immediate execution at the current market price, with no price guarantee."},{"q":"What characterises a Limit order?","o":["It fills only based on the broker's own internal decision","It fills only at a specified price or better","It always guarantees immediate execution at any price","It never fills under any market conditions"],"e":"A Limit order fills only at a specified price or a more favourable one."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of a Stop order?","o":["Automatically reducing the broker's commission","Deliberately and artificially increasing the number of daily trades","Realising an immediate, guaranteed profit in every case","Limiting a loss or locking in a profit when a level is reached"],"e":"The purpose of a Stop order is to limit a loss or lock in a profit when a given level is reached."},{"q":"Why does a Stop order not guarantee the exact price?","o":["It always does, it fills exactly at the specified price without exception","Once triggered it becomes a Market order, which is exposed to slippage","Because a Stop order never fills in any market situation","Because the broker manually decides the price of every Stop order"],"e":"When triggered, a Stop order becomes a Market order, which can be exposed to slippage rather than a guaranteed price."},{"q":"When is a Limit order better than a Market order?","o":["Only when the market is officially closed that day","Never, a Market order is always the better choice","When the exact price matters more than guaranteed execution","Only when the goal is immediate execution at any price"],"e":"A Limit order is better when reaching the exact price matters more than guaranteed execution."}]},
"4": {"t":"Market Sessions and Timing","s":"When is the best and the worst time to day trade","cards":[{"t":"The three main market sessions","b":"<p>Thanks to the different exchange sessions, the global market runs practically 24 hours a day: the <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Asian, European and American</strong> sessions. Each has its own typical volatility and liquidity.</p>"},{"t":"The first hour after the open: the busiest","b":"<p>In most markets the <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">first 30–60 minutes after the open</strong> are the most volatile — this is when overnight/weekend news is priced in and trading volume is highest. Many day traders specialise specifically in this period.</p>"},{"t":"The \"lunch lull\" — the midday dead zone","b":"<p>In many markets <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">volume and volatility drop significantly</strong> around midday — this is called the \"lunch lull\". False breakouts (fakeouts) are more common in this period, and many experienced traders trade less or not at all.</p>"},{"t":"Why does your own time zone matter too?","b":"<p>If, in your local time, a market opens at dawn or late at night, <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">you have to assess realistically whether you can trade alert and focused</strong> at that time — fatigue is one of the biggest, most underestimated risk factors in day trading.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What characterises the first hour after the open?","o":["The broker's maintenance window, with no trading at all","An official, mandatory break for all traders","Low volatility, calm and predictable movement everywhere","High volatility and volume due to accumulated information"],"e":"The first hour after the open is typically highly volatile, because the market is processing the overnight news."},{"q":"What characterises the midday dead zone?","o":["Lower volume and smaller price moves","The time when the day's biggest, most important news is released","Extremely high volume and volatility across the market","The official, mandatory close of every asset at once"],"e":"Around midday volume is typically lower and price moves are smaller."},{"q":"Why is the period around the close important?","o":["Because all trading activity always stops completely then","Because that is when the broker calculates the daily commission","It is not, it is the same as any other time of day","Many institutions close or adjust positions, increasing volume"],"e":"Around the close many institutions close or adjust positions, which leads to increased volume."},{"q":"Why is a session overlap (e.g. London–New York) relevant?","o":["Because that is the only time any asset can be traded at all","Because the market officially and mandatorily stops then","Two big markets being active at the same time can bring high liquidity","It is not, sessions never affect liquidity"],"e":"During session overlaps (e.g. London and New York) the activity of two big markets can bring high liquidity."},{"q":"Why shouldn't you necessarily trade the same way at every time of day?","o":["Because brokers forbid trading at certain times everywhere","Liquidity and volatility can differ significantly by time of day","You always should, the time of day does not matter","Because the market is physically closed for most of the day everywhere"],"e":"Liquidity and volatility can differ significantly by time of day, which affects how well a strategy works."}]},
"21": {"t":"Scalping vs Day Trading vs Swing Trading","s":"Which time frame suits you?","cards":[{"t":"Scalping: the fastest style","b":"<p><strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Scalping</strong> opens and closes positions within seconds to minutes, hunting for many tiny profits. It demands extremely intense concentration and fast reactions, and can involve high transaction costs because of the many orders.</p>"},{"t":"Day Trading: the intraday balance","b":"<p><strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Day trading</strong> works on a minutes-to-hours time frame, but always closes everything by the end of the day. It is less stressful than scalping, but still requires active, daily presence.</p>"},{"t":"Swing Trading: from days to weeks","b":"<p><strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Swing trading</strong> holds positions for days, even weeks, targeting larger trend moves. It needs less daily attention, but overnight/weekend gap risk is higher, since positions stay open.</p>"},{"t":"Which style suits you?","b":"<p>The choice depends on <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">your schedule, risk tolerance and personality</strong> — if you cannot sit in front of the screen all day, swing trading is more realistic; if you enjoy fast, intense decision-making, scalping or day trading suits you better. There is no \"better\" style, only one that fits you more or less.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What characterises scalping?","o":["Extremely short holding times, often within minutes","An exclusively long-term investment strategy held for years","Trading the market only once a week, rarely","Patiently holding positions for weeks or months"],"e":"Scalping means extremely short positions, often held only for minutes, aiming for many small profits."},{"q":"How is swing trading different from day trading?","o":["Swing trading holds positions for days to weeks, overnight too","Day trading always lasts longer than swing trading","They only differ in the indicators used","Swing trading lasts minutes, day trading lasts days"],"e":"Swing trading holds positions from days to weeks, overnight too, unlike day trading."},{"q":"Which style requires the most active attention?","o":["Scalping requires the most continuous, immediate attention","Swing trading requires the most continuous, immediate attention","None of them needs any particular attention, all are passive","Long-term investing requires the most daily attention"],"e":"Scalping requires the most continuous, immediate attention because of the fast, small moves."},{"q":"Why can scalping have higher transaction costs?","o":["With many frequent trades, the spread/commission adds up","It cannot, every style always costs the same","Because brokers charge scalpers punitively higher fees","Because scalping always means lower-volume trading"],"e":"Scalping means many frequent trades, where the cost of spread and commission can add up significantly."},{"q":"Which style suits people with less free time better?","o":["None of them, they all always require the same time","Swing trading, because it does not require continuous intraday attention","Scalping, because it requires less attention","Day trading, because it takes the least time overall"],"e":"Swing trading suits people with less free time better, because it does not require continuous attention."}]},
"22": {"t":"Choosing a Broker for Day Trading","s":"What to look out for before you start trading","cards":[{"t":"Why does execution speed matter?","b":"<p>In day trading even seconds count — a <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">broker with slow execution</strong> can fill your trade at a significantly worse price than you saw on screen (slippage), especially in volatile markets.</p>"},{"t":"Commission vs spread-based pricing","b":"<p>Some brokers charge a <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">fixed commission</strong> per trade, others <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">build their fee into the spread</strong> (the difference between buy and sell prices) — with many trades per day, commission-based pricing often works out cheaper.</p>"},{"t":"Depth of Market (Level 2)","b":"<p>More serious platforms show <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Level 2 data</strong> — not just the best bid/ask price, but the full order book behind them, which helps judge the real liquidity at a given level.</p>"},{"t":"Why is it important to try a demo/test account?","b":"<p>Before trading with real money on a platform, it is worth <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">testing the execution quality and the interface in a demo account</strong> — a familiar, fast interface significantly reduces the chance of mistakes in stressful market moments.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What is the primary criterion when choosing a broker?","o":["Only the number of the broker's social media followers","Regulatory status and protection of client funds","Only the platform's visual design and colours","Only the geographic location of the broker's headquarters"],"e":"The primary criterion should be the broker's regulatory status and the protection of client funds."},{"q":"Why does execution speed matter?","o":["In a volatile market, slow execution can result in a worse price","Because this criterion is only relevant for long-term investors","Because speed only affects the broker's commission","It does not, every broker is always equally fast"],"e":"In a volatile market, slow execution can result in a worse, less favourable price."},{"q":"Why is it worth comparing fee structures?","o":["Because the fee structure never affects the final result","With frequent trading, fee differences add up significantly","It is not, every broker charges exactly the same fees","Because the law requires identical fees at every broker"],"e":"Because of frequent day trading, even small fee differences can add up significantly over time."},{"q":"Why is the availability of a demo account important?","o":["Because the law requires every broker to offer a demo everywhere","It is not, it is always best to start live straight away","It allows risk-free testing of the platform and the strategy","Because a demo account is always exactly the same as live trading"],"e":"A demo account allows risk-free testing of how the platform works and of the strategy."},{"q":"Why is it worth checking the availability of customer support?","o":["Because customer support is only relevant during registration","Because the broker always solves everything automatically, without humans","In case of a technical problem you may need fast help","It is not, you will never need customer support"],"e":"In case of a technical problem or an urgent question, fast and available customer support can be important."}]},
"23": {"t":"The Concept of Slippage","s":"When your order is not filled at the price you expected","cards":[{"t":"What is slippage?","b":"<p><strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Slippage</strong> is the difference between the price you expected when placing the order and the actual execution price — the market \"moves\" in the moments between sending the order and its fill.</p>"},{"t":"When is the risk of slippage highest?","b":"<p>Slippage is highest <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">in volatile markets, during news, or with low-liquidity assets</strong> — at such times the market can move quickly between the order arriving and being filled.</p>"},{"t":"Market vs Limit orders in terms of slippage","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Market order</strong> guarantees execution but not the price — here the slippage risk is higher. A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Limit order</strong> guarantees the price (or better), but not execution.</p>"},{"t":"How can you reduce the impact of slippage?","b":"<p>Choosing more liquid assets, avoiding the most volatile news moments, and <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">using limit orders</strong> in volatile moments can all reduce the negative impact of slippage — although it can never be eliminated completely.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What is slippage?","o":["The difference between the expected and the actual execution price","The name for the daily price change","A chart indicator","The fixed distance between the stop-loss and the take-profit"],"e":"Slippage is the difference between the expected and the actual execution price."},{"q":"When is the risk of slippage highest?","o":["It never happens","Only at weekends","In a calm, low-volume period","In volatile markets, during news, or with low liquidity"],"e":"Slippage is highest in volatile markets, during news or with low liquidity."},{"q":"Why is slippage higher with a Market order?","o":["It is not, it is the same for both","It always guarantees a better price","A Limit order never fills","It guarantees execution, but not the price"],"e":"A Market order guarantees execution at any price, so the slippage risk is higher."},{"q":"How can the impact of slippage be reduced?","o":["With liquid assets and limit orders","It can never be reduced","By always trading during news","Only by trading illiquid assets"],"e":"Using more liquid assets and limit orders can reduce the impact of slippage."},{"q":"Why can it not be eliminated completely?","o":["Because it is only a theoretical concept","Because the market can keep moving while the order is being processed","Because it only happens with crypto","Because brokers cause it on purpose"],"e":"Slippage cannot be eliminated completely, because the market can move between sending the order and its fill."}]},
"24": {"t":"The Concept of a Short Squeeze","s":"When short sellers forced to cover magnify a rally","cards":[{"t":"What is a short squeeze?","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#FF3D5A\">short squeeze</strong> happens when the price of a heavily shorted asset suddenly starts to rise, and those in short positions are forced to buy back (cover) their positions, which pushes the price up even further.</p>"},{"t":"Why is this process self-reinforcing?","b":"<p>As the price rises, more and more short sellers fall into loss and are <strong style=\"color:#FF3D5A\">forced to cover</strong> (buy back) — this buying pressure drives the price higher, which forces even more short sellers to cover.</p>"},{"t":"What signs suggest short squeeze potential?","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#FF3D5A\">high short interest (share of shorted stock)</strong> and a low \"days to cover\" (how many days it would take to cover all shorts at average volume) can indicate that an asset may be prone to a squeeze.</p>"},{"t":"Why is this risky on both sides?","b":"<p>Short sellers face a <strong style=\"color:#FF3D5A\">theoretically unlimited loss</strong> if a squeeze develops. At the same time, anyone who jumps in Long at the top of the squeeze out of FOMO can easily become a victim of the fastest, sharpest drop when the squeeze runs out of steam.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What is a short squeeze?","o":["A sudden price rise that forces short positions to close","When buyers suddenly pull out and the price starts to plunge","A slow, gradual price rise over a long time","A slow, gradual price decline over a longer period"],"e":"A short squeeze is a sudden price rise that forces short sellers to close their positions."},{"q":"Why is the short squeeze process self-reinforcing?","o":["Forced buying drives the price higher, forcing others to close as well","Because the broker manually stops the process every time","Because a short squeeze never really affects the market price","It is not, it always fades quickly on its own"],"e":"Forced closing (buying) drives the price higher, which forces more short sellers to close."},{"q":"With which assets is a short squeeze more common?","o":["With the largest stocks that have the lowest short interest","Only with the largest, most stable blue-chip stocks","Only with government bonds, never with other assets","With heavily shorted, lower-liquidity assets"],"e":"A short squeeze is more common with heavily shorted, lower-liquidity assets."},{"q":"Why is it risky to hold a short position on such an asset?","o":["Because the broker always guarantees the loss is limited automatically","A sudden, sharp rise can cause an unlimited loss","It is not, a short position is always a safe investment","Because a short squeeze can always be predicted precisely in advance"],"e":"A sudden, sharp price rise can cause an unlimited loss on a short position without a stop-loss."},{"q":"What is the best protection against short squeeze risk?","o":["Using a strict stop-loss on short positions too","Completely ignoring position sizing in every case","Deliberately dropping the stop-loss on short positions","Deliberately and significantly increasing the short position size"],"e":"Using a strict stop-loss on short positions too is essential against squeeze risk."}]},
"25": {"t":"Trading Halt — a Pause in Market Trading","s":"When the exchange temporarily suspends trading","cards":[{"t":"What is a trading halt?","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">trading halt</strong> is a temporary, official suspension of trading in a given asset — typically ordered because of extreme price moves, important expected news, or a technical problem.</p>"},{"t":"What types of halts are there?","b":"<p>There are <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">volatility halts</strong> (when the price moves too fast within a short time) and <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">news halts</strong> (when a company makes an important announcement and the exchange gives the market time to process it).</p>"},{"t":"What happens to open positions during a halt?","b":"<p>During a halt <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">you can neither open nor close positions</strong> in that asset — if you have an open position, it is \"frozen\" until trading resumes, which can be a significant risk.</p>"},{"t":"Why is this important to know as a day trader?","b":"<p>The reopening after a halt can be <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">extremely volatile</strong> — the price can jump hugely in either direction, as accumulated orders try to fill all at once. This is especially dangerous if the halt caught you without a stop-loss or in an open position.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What is a trading halt?","o":["A type of order","A temporary suspension of trading in an asset","The broker's annual holiday","The official name of the daily close"],"e":"A trading halt is a temporary, official suspension of trading in an asset."},{"q":"What are the two main types of halts?","o":["Volatility halts and news halts","Only weekend halts","Only morning and evening halts","There is only one kind of halt"],"e":"There are volatility halts and news halts."},{"q":"What happens to open positions during a halt?","o":["Nothing changes","They cannot be traded, they are 'frozen'","They automatically double","They are closed automatically"],"e":"During a halt the asset cannot be traded; the open position is frozen for the duration of the stoppage."},{"q":"What risk does the reopening after a halt pose?","o":["It always continues at exactly the same price","No risk, trading always simply continues","It can be extremely volatile, with a big jump in either direction","It is only a formal event"],"e":"The reopening after a halt can be extremely volatile, because accumulated orders try to fill at the same time."},{"q":"Why is this dangerous if the halt catches you in an open position?","o":["It is not, it is always safe","It is only a formal concern","It guarantees a profit","You cannot react during the halt while the risk remains"],"e":"During a halt you cannot react to your open position while the risk (e.g. the jump at reopening) remains."}]},
"38": {"t":"Reading the Order Book","s":"A live map of buying and selling intentions","cards":[{"t":"What is the order book?","b":"<p>The <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">order book</strong> shows, in real time, all pending buy (bid) and sell (ask) orders for an asset, sorted by price.</p>"},{"t":"The bid side and the ask side","b":"<p>The <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">bid side</strong> shows those who want to buy (and at what price), the <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">ask side</strong> those who want to sell. The difference between the best bid and the best ask is the spread.</p>"},{"t":"What can a large order (wall) in the book mean?","b":"<p>An unusually <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">large buy or sell order</strong> (a \"wall\") at a given price level can signal psychological support/resistance — but be careful: these orders often disappear just as quickly (a suspected spoofing tactic).</p>"},{"t":"Why shouldn't you trust the order book blindly?","b":"<p>The order book <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">can be manipulated</strong> — some participants place large orders they never intend to fill (\"spoofing\") to influence others' decisions, then withdraw them as the price approaches.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What does the order book show?","o":["A list of daily closing prices","A chronological list of already closed trades","Only the daily open and close, no other data","Pending buy and sell orders by price"],"e":"The order book shows pending buy (bid) and sell (ask) orders, sorted by price."},{"q":"What can a large buy 'wall' at a given level mean?","o":["A technical fault in the broker's system","Always an immediate, guaranteed price drop within the next minute","Significant buying interest, potential support at that level","Nothing, it is always a random, meaningless coincidence in the book"],"e":"A large buy 'wall' can signal significant buying interest, acting as potential support at that level."},{"q":"Why can the order book sometimes be misleading?","o":["Because the broker always edits the displayed data manually","It cannot, it always reflects real intentions exactly","Large orders can be withdrawn quickly, only creating an appearance","Because the order book never shows real, current data"],"e":"Large orders can be withdrawn quickly (spoofing), creating only a temporary, misleading appearance in the market."},{"q":"What is the role of the bid-ask spread in the order book?","o":["It is the total aggregated daily trading volume","It has none, it is a completely separate, unrelated concept","It shows the exact daily amount of the broker's commission in forints","It shows the difference between the best buy and sell price"],"e":"The bid-ask spread shows the difference between the best bid and the best ask price in the order book."},{"q":"Why is it hard to read the order book in real time?","o":["Because the order book never updates in real time","It is not, anyone can understand it instantly without practice","Because only the broker's employees can access this data","It changes quickly and requires practice and advanced pattern recognition"],"e":"The order book changes quickly, so reading it in real time takes practice and advanced pattern recognition."}]},
"39": {"t":"The Bid-Ask Spread","s":"The hidden cost behind every trade","cards":[{"t":"What is the bid-ask spread?","b":"<p>The <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">spread</strong> is the difference between the best buy price (bid) and the best sell price (ask) — it is one of the market's basic, hidden trading costs, even without commission.</p>"},{"t":"Why do you pay the spread on every trade?","b":"<p>If you buy an asset immediately at the ask price and then wanted to sell it right away, you would get the bid price back — the <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">difference between the two is an automatic loss</strong>, before any price movement happens.</p>"},{"t":"Why is the spread wider for illiquid assets?","b":"<p>With low-volume assets <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">fewer market participants compete</strong> for the best price, so market makers keep a wider spread to cover their own risk.</p>"},{"t":"Why does this matter especially in day trading?","b":"<p>Because a day trader <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">makes many trades in a short time</strong>, the cost of the spread adds up — choosing a liquid asset with a tight spread can significantly improve long-term profitability.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What is the bid-ask spread?","o":["An indicator that precisely measures daily momentum","The broker's annual maintenance fee in forints","The difference between the best buy and sell price","The exact size of the daily price change"],"e":"The spread is the difference between the best buy (bid) and the best sell (ask) price."},{"q":"Why is the spread an automatic cost?","o":["Buying at the ask, you could only sell immediately at the bid, which is a loss","It is not a cost, just a theoretical concept","It only matters in forex, never in other markets","It always guarantees a profit on every trade"],"e":"If you bought and immediately sold the asset, the bid-ask difference would cause an immediate loss."},{"q":"Why is the spread wider for illiquid assets?","o":["Because illiquid assets are always more expensive than any other asset","Fewer participants compete, and market makers cover their risk","Because the broker deliberately penalises illiquid assets","It is not, it is always exactly the same for every asset"],"e":"With low-volume assets there are fewer competitors, so market makers keep a wider spread."},{"q":"Why does the spread matter especially in day trading?","o":["It is only a theoretical issue, negligible in practice","With many trades, the cost of the spread adds up significantly","It is only relevant for stocks, not for other assets","It does not, it only matters for long-term investors"],"e":"A day trader makes many trades, so the cost of the spread can add up significantly."},{"q":"What kind of asset is worth choosing to minimise spread costs?","o":["Liquid assets with a tight spread","Always the most expensive, most exclusive assets on the market","Specifically the least liquid, least frequently traded assets","An asset's liquidity does not matter here"],"e":"Choosing liquid assets with a tight spread reduces the cost caused by the spread."}]},
"40": {"t":"Market Liquidity","s":"How easily you can get in and out without a loss","cards":[{"t":"What is liquidity?","b":"<p><strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Liquidity</strong> measures how easily (and with how small a price impact) you can buy or sell an asset — with high liquidity there are many buyers/sellers, allowing fast trades with little slippage.</p>"},{"t":"How do you assess an asset's liquidity?","b":"<p><strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Average daily trading volume and the width of the bid-ask spread</strong> are the two most important measures — high volume and a tight spread indicate high liquidity.</p>"},{"t":"Why is it dangerous to day trade an illiquid asset?","b":"<p>With illiquid assets <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">a larger position can move the price itself</strong>, the spread is wider, and it is harder to exit quickly if the market turns against you — all of which magnifies the risk.</p>"},{"t":"Why can liquidity drop during the day?","b":"<p>Liquidity is not constant — it is typically <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">highest around the open and the close</strong>, while it can drop significantly during the quieter midday hours, causing wider spreads and more slippage.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What is liquidity?","o":["How easily you can buy/sell with little price impact","The registration fee charged by the broker","A company's total annual profit and loss","The total number of trades per day"],"e":"Liquidity measures how easily, and with how little price impact, an asset can be traded."},{"q":"Which two main measures indicate liquidity?","o":["Only the dividend yield","Only the company's market capitalisation","Only the daily change in the share price","Average daily volume and the width of the spread"],"e":"Average daily trading volume and the width of the bid-ask spread are the two most important liquidity measures."},{"q":"Why is it dangerous to day trade an illiquid asset?","o":["A larger position can move the price, the spread is wider, exiting is harder","Because illiquid assets never move in any direction","It is not, it is always safer","Because illiquid assets are always more expensive than their peers"],"e":"With illiquid assets a larger position can move the price, the spread is wider and it is harder to exit quickly."},{"q":"When is a market's liquidity highest during the day?","o":["Always evenly spread over the whole day","Exactly during the midday dead zone","Only late at night, when few people trade","Around the open and the close"],"e":"Liquidity is typically highest around the open and the close, while it can drop around midday."},{"q":"What can happen if liquidity drops suddenly?","o":["Nothing, the spread always stays the same","The market automatically stops immediately in every case","The quality of trading automatically improves completely","Wider spreads and a higher slippage risk can develop"],"e":"As liquidity drops, the spread can widen and the risk of slippage can increase."}]},
"41": {"t":"Pre-Market and After-Hours Trading","s":"When the market is 'officially' closed, but you can still trade","cards":[{"t":"What are pre-market and after-hours trading?","b":"<p>Many exchanges also allow trading in the <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">pre-market</strong> period before the official open and the <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">after-hours</strong> period after the close, although with limited volume and different rules.</p>"},{"t":"Why is liquidity lower in these periods?","b":"<p>Far <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">fewer market participants are active</strong> in the pre-market/after-hours sessions than during regular trading hours — this leads to wider spreads and more volatility for the same order size.</p>"},{"t":"Why does this matter for news?","b":"<p>Many companies publish their earnings <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">right after the close or before the open</strong> — at such times the pre-market/after-hours price can differ sharply from the previous close, which can cause a serious gap at the next regular open.</p>"},{"t":"What extra risk does this pose for traders?","b":"<p>Because of the low liquidity, <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">the risk of slippage is much higher</strong>, and many brokers apply stricter rules (e.g. limit orders only) in these periods, precisely because of the unpredictability.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What characterises pre-market trading?","o":["A lower-liquidity trading period before the official open","The most active, highest-volume period after the official open","The day's biggest, highest-volume period in every case","The high-liquidity period after the official close"],"e":"Pre-market is the trading period before the official open, typically with lower liquidity."},{"q":"Why is the spread wider in these periods?","o":["Fewer market participants are active, liquidity is lower","It is not, it is exactly the same as in normal trading hours","Because the law requires a wider spread at these times","Because the broker always deliberately widens the spread then"],"e":"Fewer market participants are active in these periods, which leads to lower liquidity and a wider spread."},{"q":"Why is trading riskier in these periods?","o":["Because the market is officially closed, so all trading is illegal then","Because of low liquidity, the risk of slippage and volatility is higher","Because the broker never fills orders in these hours","It is not, it is always safer than normal trading"],"e":"Because of the low liquidity the risk of slippage is higher, and volatility can be more unpredictable."},{"q":"Why is it important to watch pre-market moves before the open?","o":["Because the pre-market move always repeats exactly during the day","Because it only serves an aesthetic, decorative purpose in chart analysis","It is not, pre-market never affects the open","It can show how the market reacted to overnight news"],"e":"The pre-market move can show how the market reacted to overnight news, giving context for the open."},{"q":"Which brokers typically allow this kind of trading?","o":["No broker anywhere in the world ever allows it","Only certain brokers that specifically offer it, with restrictions","Only those dealing exclusively in cryptocurrencies","Every broker automatically, without exceptions or restrictions"],"e":"Only certain brokers that specifically offer it allow pre-market/after-hours trading, often with restrictions."}]},
"54": {"t":"Types of Market Participants","s":"Who you are really competing with in the market","cards":[{"t":"Market makers","b":"<p><strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Market makers</strong> continuously quote prices on both sides (buy and sell), providing liquidity to the market — their profit typically comes from the spread, not from direction.</p>"},{"t":"Institutional investors","b":"<p><strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Institutional players</strong> (funds, banks) move huge sizes — they often split their orders into many parts (order slicing) so as not to move the price too much with a single transaction.</p>"},{"t":"Algorithmic/HFT traders","b":"<p><strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Algorithmic and high-frequency (HFT) traders</strong> make decisions within milliseconds — they account for a large part of daily volume in many markets and significantly influence short-term price behaviour.</p>"},{"t":"Retail traders","b":"<p><strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Retail traders</strong> (like an individual day trader) are the smallest but most numerous market participants — it is important to realise that you are competing in a shared marketplace with the players above, who have far greater resources.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"Who are market makers?","o":["Only government bodies that regulate the market from outside","Only private individuals trading with their own small capital","The broker's customer service staff who provide help","Participants who continuously quote buy and sell prices"],"e":"Market makers continuously quote buy and sell prices, providing liquidity to the market."},{"q":"Why are institutional investors important for the market?","o":["With their large capital they can significantly influence price moves","Because they are the only ones with access to market data","Because the law requires them to trade every day","They are not, the market is moved exclusively by retail traders"],"e":"With their large capital, institutional investors can significantly influence price moves in the market."},{"q":"What characterises retail traders among market participants?","o":["They set the official regulation of exchanges everywhere","They have the most capital of all market participants","A group of individual traders and investors with smaller capital","They are the only ones who continuously quote buy and sell prices"],"e":"Retail traders are the group of individual investors and traders with smaller capital in the market."},{"q":"Why is it worth knowing the motivations of different participants?","o":["Because the law requires every trader to acquire this knowledge","It is not, every participant behaves exactly the same","Because only this decides who officially controls the market","It can help understand the logic behind certain market moves"],"e":"Knowing the motivations of different participants can help understand the logic behind certain market moves."},{"q":"Why do large institutions sometimes move together?","o":["Because a single player secretly controls all the others","Because the law requires coordinated, joint decision-making","Similar models and strategies can lead to similar conclusions","They never move together, they always decide completely independently"],"e":"Because of similar analytical models and strategies, large institutions sometimes reach similar conclusions and move together."}]},
"55": {"t":"Building a Watchlist","s":"Why you can't watch everything at once","cards":[{"t":"What is a watchlist and why do you need one?","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">watchlist</strong> is a narrowed-down list of the assets you actively follow and potentially plan to trade on a given day — there are thousands of assets on the market, and it is impossible to handle them all at once.</p>"},{"t":"What criteria should you select by?","b":"<p>It is worth including assets that <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">fit your strategy</strong> — e.g. liquid enough, with suitable volatility, and perhaps with some catalyst (news, earnings) around them that day.</p>"},{"t":"Why prepare it in advance, in the evening/morning?","b":"<p>Building your watchlist <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">in advance, in a calm state</strong> is a much better decision than hastily searching for new opportunities while trading — this is also part of disciplined planning.</p>"},{"t":"Why shouldn't the list be too long?","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">watchlist that is too long</strong> causes the same problem as having none at all — you cannot really watch them all closely, and superficial attention can lead to bad decisions.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What is the main purpose of a watchlist?","o":["Measuring the total aggregated daily trading volume","Calculating the day's profit in advance","Determining the exact official opening and closing times of the market","Building a list of preselected assets to watch"],"e":"The purpose of a watchlist is to preselect and build a list of assets to watch for the day."},{"q":"Why is it better to build it in advance than to rush after the open?","o":["Because the broker officially only accepts a list prepared in advance","Because it is never possible to select any asset after the open","A decision made in a calm, analytical state is more reliable than rushing","It is not, an immediate decision after the open is always most effective"],"e":"A decision made in advance, in a calm, analytical state, is more reliable than rushing after the open."},{"q":"What criteria are worth filtering assets by?","o":["Randomly, without any specific criteria","Only by the mandatory list recommended by the broker","Liquidity, volatility and relevance to your own strategy","Only by the company's name, no other criterion is relevant"],"e":"It is worth filtering assets by liquidity, volatility and relevance to your own strategy."},{"q":"Why shouldn't your daily watchlist be too long?","o":["Because the broker always limits the number of assets on the list","It can be any length, the more assets the better","Because a long list automatically slows the platform down","Watching too many assets at once makes quality analysis harder"],"e":"Watching too many assets at once can make proper, thorough analysis of each one harder."},{"q":"When is it worth updating the watchlist?","o":["Never, once built it must stay unchanged forever","Only once a month, on the first day of the month","Regularly, for example as part of your daily pre-market routine","Only when the broker specifically asks the trader to"],"e":"It is worth updating the watchlist regularly, for example as part of the daily pre-market routine."}]},
"56": {"t":"Tick Size and Lot Size","s":"The smallest units of trading","cards":[{"t":"What is tick size?","b":"<p><strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Tick size</strong> is the smallest possible price movement for a given asset — e.g. for many stocks it is $0.01, but it can differ by asset and exchange.</p>"},{"t":"What is lot size?","b":"<p><strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Lot size</strong> is the minimum quantity unit in which an asset can be traded — e.g. in forex the position size is often counted in \"lots\" (standard, mini, micro).</p>"},{"t":"Why does this matter for position sizing?","b":"<p>If you do not know an asset's <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">tick and lot size</strong> exactly, you can easily miscalculate your real risk and your potential profit/loss on a position.</p>"},{"t":"Why do these differ between assets?","b":"<p>Tick and lot sizes are <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">set by the exchange or the regulator</strong> for each asset — it is worth checking these basic details for every new asset you start trading.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What is tick size?","o":["The total aggregated size of the daily price change","The smallest possible unit of price movement","The official, predetermined length of the trading day","The minimum, mandatory size of the broker's commission"],"e":"Tick size is the smallest possible unit of price movement for a given asset."},{"q":"What is lot size?","o":["The exact length of the market's official opening hours","The amount of the annual maintenance fee charged by the broker","The standard, standardised quantity unit of trading","The smallest possible price movement of an asset"],"e":"Lot size is the standard, standardised quantity unit of trading in a given market."},{"q":"Why is it important to know the dollar value of a tick?","o":["You do not need it, this is completely unnecessary data for everyone","Because the law requires every trader to memorise it","It is only relevant for the broker, not for the trader at all","Without it you cannot calculate profit/loss accurately"],"e":"Knowing the dollar value of a tick is essential for calculating profit/loss accurately."},{"q":"What happens if you don't know the lot size of a new asset?","o":["The market automatically stops until you learn it precisely","You can misjudge the real position size and risk","No problem can ever arise, it is always automatic","The broker always automatically corrects the mistake for you"],"e":"If you don't know the lot size, you can misjudge the real position size and risk."},{"q":"Why can tick size differ between assets?","o":["The exchange standardises this value differently for each asset","Because the broker always sets it randomly and arbitrarily","It cannot, it is exactly the same for every asset","Because this concept only exists in the forex market"],"e":"Tick size can differ between assets because the exchange standardises this value for each asset separately."}]},
"57": {"t":"Taxes on Day Trading Profits","s":"What you need to know before planning to spend your profit","cards":[{"t":"Why is this important even before you start trading?","b":"<p>Day trading profit is <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">taxable income</strong> — it is worth knowing the rules that apply to you before you start trading, rather than finding out afterwards as a surprise.</p>"},{"t":"Why can the rules differ by asset?","b":"<p>In many countries, profits from stock trading, cryptocurrency trading or leveraged CFD trading are <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">taxed differently</strong> — it is worth looking up the specific rules that apply to you.</p>"},{"t":"Why is it worth documenting every trade?","b":"<p>Keeping <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">an accurate record of every trade</strong> (date, price, quantity, fees) is not only useful for your trading journal, it can also be essential for preparing your tax return.</p>"},{"t":"Why does this lesson not replace professional advice?","b":"<p>Tax laws <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">vary by country and change over time</strong> — this lesson only draws your attention to the topic; for the exact, current rules always consult a tax advisor or accountant.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"Why is it important to know the tax rules as a day trader?","o":["Because taxation only applies to long-term investors","Because all tax matters are the broker's responsibility, not the trader's","Profits are typically taxable, which affects the net result","It is not, day trading is always tax-free"],"e":"Day trading profits are typically taxable, which directly affects the real, net result."},{"q":"Why can tax rules differ by country?","o":["Because day trading is not regulated for tax purposes anywhere","Tax laws are a national competence and are regulated differently","They cannot, exactly the same rules apply everywhere","Because the broker can freely decide what tax has to be paid"],"e":"Tax laws are a national competence, so they can differ significantly from country to country."},{"q":"Why is it worth consulting an expert on this question?","o":["It is not, taxation is always simple and clear for everyone","The exact rules can be complex, and a mistake can have serious consequences","Because experts always give the same information as the internet","Because the law forbids day traders from preparing their own tax return"],"e":"The exact tax rules can be complex, and a mistake can have serious financial consequences."},{"q":"Why is a trading journal also useful for your tax return?","o":["There is never any connection between the journal and the tax return","It gives an accurate, documented record of profits and losses","Because the journal automatically fills in the tax return for us","Because the broker never issues an official annual statement"],"e":"A carefully kept journal gives an accurate, documented record of profits and losses for the tax return."},{"q":"Why shouldn't you put off thinking about tax matters?","o":["You can safely put it off, it is never urgent","Because putting it off always reduces the final tax due","Accumulated, unresolved tax matters are harder to handle later","Because the tax authority never checks day traders' returns"],"e":"Accumulated, unresolved tax matters become harder and harder to handle over time, so it is worth dealing with them early."}]},
"70": {"t":"Maker and Taker Fees","s":"Why you sometimes get a rebate and sometimes pay a fee","cards":[{"t":"What is a maker fee?","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">maker</strong> is someone who adds liquidity to the market — e.g. with a limit order that does not fill immediately but waits in the order book. Many platforms reward this with a lower fee (sometimes a rebate).</p>"},{"t":"What is a taker fee?","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">taker</strong> is someone who removes liquidity from the market — e.g. with a market order that fills immediately against existing orders. This typically comes with a higher fee.</p>"},{"t":"Why does this matter in day trading?","b":"<p>Because a day trader <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">makes many trades</strong>, the maker/taker fee difference can add up significantly — consciously using limit orders can noticeably reduce costs over time.</p>"},{"t":"Why is the maker strategy not always better?","b":"<p>A maker position (limit order) <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">does not guarantee execution</strong> — if the price does not reach your level you can miss the move, while a taker (market) order always fills immediately, just for a higher fee.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What is a maker fee?","o":["The fee paid for an immediate order that removes liquidity","The price of the official, mandatory upper limit on daily trades","The fee paid for a limit order that adds liquidity to the market","The broker's annual fixed maintenance fee for every client"],"e":"A maker fee is the — often lower — fee paid for orders, typically limit orders, that add liquidity to the market."},{"q":"What is a taker fee?","o":["The broker's monthly fixed maintenance fee, the same for every client","The fee charged for placing a liquidity-adding limit order","An official levy calculated from average daily volume","The fee paid for a market order that immediately removes existing liquidity"],"e":"A taker fee is the fee paid for orders, typically market orders, that immediately remove existing market liquidity."},{"q":"Why is the maker fee typically lower than the taker fee?","o":["It is not, the two fee types are always exactly the same","Because the law requires exchanges to apply identical fees","Because maker orders are always riskier for the exchange","Exchanges encourage orders that add liquidity with a lower fee"],"e":"Exchanges typically encourage orders that add liquidity (maker orders) with a lower fee."},{"q":"Why is this distinction important for active traders?","o":["Because the maker/taker difference only exists for cryptocurrencies","Because this question is only relevant for long-term investors","With many trades, the fee difference can add up significantly","It is not, the fee difference is always negligible"],"e":"With many frequent trades, the maker/taker fee difference can add up significantly, affecting the net result."},{"q":"How can you become a maker instead of a taker on a given order?","o":["By using a limit order that does not fill immediately","It is not possible, it always depends solely on the broker","You can only become a maker by using a market order","By significantly increasing your position size you automatically become a maker"],"e":"By using a limit order that adds liquidity to the market (does not fill immediately), you can become a maker."}]},
"71": {"t":"Comparing Asset Classes for Day Trading","s":"Stocks, forex, crypto or futures — which suits you?","cards":[{"t":"Stocks","b":"<p>The <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">stock market</strong> typically has limited trading hours, is often more regulated (e.g. PDT rules in the USA), and is strongly influenced by news/earnings reports.</p>"},{"t":"Forex (currency market)","b":"<p>The <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">forex market</strong> runs practically 24 hours a day on weekdays, is extremely liquid in the major currency pairs, and is strongly moved by macroeconomic news (rate decisions, inflation data).</p>"},{"t":"Cryptocurrencies","b":"<p>The <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">crypto market</strong> is open 24/7, at weekends too — this poses a unique risk (there is no \"day off\" from watching positions), and its volatility is typically higher than that of traditional assets.</p>"},{"t":"Futures","b":"<p><strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Futures markets</strong> (e.g. index futures, commodity futures) work with standardised contracts, often with a lower fee structure, but with expiration dates and different margin rules.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"Why is the volatility of stocks different from that of forex?","o":["Because forex is always far more volatile than every stock without exception","Stocks have different, individual volatility profiles per asset","Because stocks never move, they always stay at a fixed price","There is no real difference, every asset class behaves the same"],"e":"Stocks can have very different, individual volatility profiles per asset, unlike forex pairs."},{"q":"Why is crypto day trading attractive to many?","o":["Because of 24-hour trading and high volatility","Because the law requires everyone to trade only cryptocurrencies","Because cryptocurrencies never show volatility, they are always stable","Because cryptocurrencies are guaranteed to always make a profit"],"e":"Continuous, 24-hour trading and typically high volatility make it attractive to many day traders."},{"q":"Why are the transaction costs of futures often lower?","o":["Because the law forbids brokers from charging commission on them","Because of the contract structure, more favourable pricing is often available","They are not, they are always the highest of all asset classes","Because futures never require any commission"],"e":"Because of the contract structure of futures, more favourable, competitive pricing is often available."},{"q":"Why is it important to consider an asset class's liquidity too?","o":["Lower liquidity can mean a higher risk of slippage","Because liquid assets always carry a higher risk","It is not, every asset class is always equally liquid","Because liquidity only affects the broker's commission"],"e":"Lower-liquidity asset classes can mean a higher risk of slippage for the trader."},{"q":"Why shouldn't you switch asset classes often without experience?","o":["Switch freely, every asset class behaves exactly the same","Because the law forbids trading more than one asset class at a time","Each asset class has unique characteristics that need to be learned","Because the broker automatically restricts switching between asset classes"],"e":"Each asset class has unique characteristics, risks and behaviour patterns that are worth learning thoroughly."}]},
"72": {"t":"News Trading and the Economic Calendar","s":"When a data release moves the market in moments","cards":[{"t":"What is the economic calendar?","b":"<p>The <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">economic calendar</strong> shows in advance when important macroeconomic data (rate decisions, unemployment rate, inflation) will be released — their timing is known in advance, only the outcome is not.</p>"},{"t":"Why is volatility extreme at the moment of major news?","b":"<p>At the moment of an important data release, the market <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">prices in the new information almost instantly</strong> — this can cause extremely fast, sometimes unpredictable moves swinging both ways within seconds.</p>"},{"t":"Why is it risky to be in a position before/during news?","b":"<p>Around the news <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">the spread can widen and slippage can grow</strong>, and the market can at times become almost untradable — many experienced day traders deliberately avoid building active positions right before/during major news.</p>"},{"t":"\"News trading\" as a strategy of its own","b":"<p>Some deliberately build their strategy <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">specifically on the fast move after the news</strong> — a highly specialised approach that demands fast reactions and is not for everyone.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What is the main purpose of the economic calendar?","o":["Tracking important, pre-scheduled economic data releases","Forecasting the day's price highs","Forecasting daily prices","Publishing daily price forecasts"],"e":"The purpose of the economic calendar is to track important, pre-scheduled economic data releases."},{"q":"Why is the moment a major news item is announced extremely volatile?","o":["Because the broker always deliberately increases volatility then","Because the law requires markets to amplify volatility on news","It is not, it always stays calm and predictable after every announcement","The market rushes to price in the new, unexpected information"],"e":"When major news is announced, the market rushes to price in the new information, causing extreme volatility."},{"q":"Why is it risky to wait for news with an open position?","o":["Because of slippage and extreme moves, even a stop-loss does not guarantee an exact price","It is not risky, it is always safe to wait for news with an open position","Because news never affects the value of open positions","Because the broker always closes positions automatically before news"],"e":"Because of slippage and extreme moves, even a stop-loss does not guarantee an exact, predictable price during major news."},{"q":"Why do many reduce their position size before important news?","o":["Because the broker requires a size reduction before every news release","They don't, they always keep the same size in every case","To mitigate the risk of extreme volatility and slippage","Because news always brings a guaranteed profit with larger positions"],"e":"Reducing position size helps mitigate the risk of extreme volatility and slippage caused by news."},{"q":"What characterises a news trading strategy?","o":["A passive approach built entirely on avoiding news","Closing every position immediately during news and waiting","A strategy that specifically aims to exploit the fast move after the news","The official, mandatory upper limit on the total number of daily trades"],"e":"News trading is a strategy that specifically aims to exploit the fast price move after important news."}]},
"73": {"t":"Dark Pools","s":"Trading that does not show in the public order book","cards":[{"t":"What is a dark pool?","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">dark pool</strong> is a private trading venue where large institutional players can trade without their order appearing in the public order book before execution.</p>"},{"t":"Why do institutions use them?","b":"<p>A huge order on a public market <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">would move the price significantly</strong> before it was filled — a dark pool lets them avoid this, executing large transactions with smaller market impact.</p>"},{"t":"Why is this important for retail traders to know?","b":"<p>Dark pool activity means that <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">the real, full market supply/demand is not always visible</strong> in the public order book — which limits how far you can fully trust the visible data.</p>"},{"t":"What concerns are raised about dark pools?","b":"<p>Critics argue that dark pools <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">can reduce market transparency</strong> and create an uneven playing field between large institutions and smaller retail players.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What is a dark pool?","o":["The maximum leverage offered by the broker","The average daily spread for liquid assets","A private venue for institutional orders, without public visibility","A public order book"],"e":"A dark pool is a private trading venue where large institutions can trade without public visibility."},{"q":"Why do institutions use them?","o":["It is always cheaper in every case","It is required by law","Placing large orders on public markets is forbidden","To avoid the large order moving the price in advance"],"e":"A dark pool allows large transactions to be executed without moving the price in advance."},{"q":"Why does this matter for retail traders?","o":["It does not matter to them","Real supply/demand is not always visible in the public book","Retail traders can also use them without limit","The activity always shows on the chart immediately"],"e":"Dark pool activity means the full market supply/demand is not reflected in the public book."},{"q":"What criticism do dark pools face?","o":["They are only theoretical concepts","Nobody criticises them","They always improve transparency","They can reduce market transparency"],"e":"Critics argue that dark pools can reduce market transparency and create unequal competition."},{"q":"What is the main difference between a dark pool and a public exchange?","o":["It only applies to cryptocurrencies","A dark pool is always slower","Orders are not publicly visible before execution","There is no difference"],"e":"The main difference is that in a dark pool orders are not shown publicly before execution."}]},
"86": {"t":"Contract Specifications for Futures","s":"What you need to know before trading futures","cards":[{"t":"What is contract size?","b":"<p>Every futures contract has a <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">standard contract size</strong> — e.g. an oil future represents a given number of barrels, an index future a given dollar multiplier per point of movement.</p>"},{"t":"What is the expiry date?","b":"<p>Futures contracts have an <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">expiry date</strong> — if you trade them as a day trader, you need to know when the contract expires, because liquidity and behaviour can change as expiry approaches.</p>"},{"t":"Why is calculating the tick value important?","b":"<p>For every contract you need to know <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">how much one tick (the smallest price move) is worth</strong> in dollars — without this you cannot calculate the real risk of a position accurately.</p>"},{"t":"Why do these differ so much between assets?","b":"<p>Contract specifications are <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">set by the exchange</strong> for each product — always check these basics before you start trading any new futures instrument.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What is contract size?","o":["The daily price change in percent","The broker's monthly maintenance cost in forints","The standard, standardised size of a futures contract","The official length of the market's trading hours"],"e":"Contract size is the standard size of a futures contract, set by the exchange."},{"q":"Why is it important to know the expiry date?","o":["It never matters for day traders at all","Because after expiry the contract's value automatically doubles","It is only a theoretical concept with no practical effect","Liquidity and behaviour can change as expiry approaches"],"e":"As expiry approaches, the contract's liquidity and behaviour can change."},{"q":"Why do you need to know the tick value in dollars?","o":["Without it the real risk cannot be calculated accurately","You don't need it, it is completely unnecessary data","It only matters to the broker, not the trader","Because every trader is legally required to learn it by heart"],"e":"Knowing the dollar value of a tick is essential for calculating the real risk accurately."},{"q":"Who sets the contract specifications?","o":["Every trader can decide individually","They are not regulated anywhere, completely free","Only the broker, completely arbitrarily at any time","The exchange"],"e":"Contract specifications are set by the exchange for each product."},{"q":"What should you do before trading a new futures instrument?","o":["Only look at the chart colour, nothing else","Immediately open a maximum position without thinking","Check the contract specifications","Ignore this data completely"],"e":"It is always worth checking a contract's specifications before you start trading it."}]},
"87": {"t":"Options Basics for Day Traders","s":"Calls and puts — a short introduction to a more complex world","cards":[{"t":"What is a call option?","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">call option</strong> gives the right (but not the obligation) to buy an asset at a set price (the strike price) until a given date — it is profitable if the price rises above the strike.</p>"},{"t":"What is a put option?","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">put option</strong> is the opposite: it gives the right to sell at a set price — it is profitable if the price falls below the strike.</p>"},{"t":"Why is options day trading attractive to some?","b":"<p>Options offer <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">built-in leverage</strong> — with relatively little capital you can control a position with a large notional value, which magnifies both the potential profit and the potential loss.</p>"},{"t":"Why is this more complex than a simple Long/Short?","b":"<p>An option's price is influenced <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">not only by the price of the underlying asset</strong> but also by the passage of time (time decay) and by volatility — this gives a much more complex risk profile that requires serious, separate study.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What is a call option?","o":["An obligation to sell the asset at a set price","The right to buy the asset at a set price within a set time","The right to sell the asset at a set price, with no deadline","The right to sell the asset at a set price"],"e":"A call option gives the right (but not the obligation) to buy the asset at a set price within a set time."},{"q":"What is a put option?","o":["The right to sell the asset at a set price within a set time","An obligation to buy the asset","An obligation to purchase the asset at a set price","The right to buy the asset at a set price"],"e":"A put option gives the right to sell the asset at a set price within a set time."},{"q":"What is the maximum loss on a bought option?","o":["Limited to the premium paid","Always exactly the full current market value of the underlying asset","Theoretically unlimited, it can grow indefinitely depending on the price move","A bought option position never carries any real, practical risk"],"e":"With a bought (long) option the maximum loss is limited to the premium paid."},{"q":"Why is options trading more complex than stock trading?","o":["Extra factors (expiry, volatility) must also be taken into account","It is not more complex, both are exactly as simple","Because options trading is only available to institutions","Because options never move together with the underlying asset's price"],"e":"Options trading also involves extra factors (time to expiry, implied volatility), which makes it more complex."},{"q":"Why is it risky to write (sell) an option without cover?","o":["Because a sold option always expires worthless automatically","Because the buyer pays the maximum loss","It is not risky, a sold option never carries any real risk","It can cause a theoretically unlimited loss if the price moves a lot"],"e":"An option written without cover can cause a theoretically unlimited loss if the price moves strongly against it."}]},
"88": {"t":"Controlling Slippage with Order Settings","s":"Practical use of the limit price and maximum slippage tolerance","cards":[{"t":"What is the role of the limit price in a stop order?","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Stop-Limit order</strong> lets you specify a maximum price at which you are still willing to accept execution — this limits slippage, but also carries a risk (see the earlier lesson).</p>"},{"t":"What is \"maximum slippage tolerance\" on some platforms?","b":"<p>Some (mainly crypto) platforms let you <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">explicitly set a maximum slippage %</strong> — if the market moved more than that between sending and executing the order, the order is not filled.</p>"},{"t":"Why does this protection have a price?","b":"<p>Strict slippage limits risk that <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">your order is not filled at all</strong> during a fast market move — you have to find the balance between protection and certainty of execution yourself.</p>"},{"t":"When should you use stricter or looser settings?","b":"<p>In a calm, liquid market a <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">stricter tolerance</strong> is sensible; in a volatile, news-heavy period it can be worth loosening it if you definitely want your order filled, even at a slightly worse price.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"How can slippage be reduced with a limit order?","o":["It significantly increases execution speed in every case","It always guarantees an instant fill at whatever price","It cannot, a limit order is always just as risky","It sets a maximum acceptable price for execution"],"e":"A limit order sets a maximum acceptable price, limiting the size of slippage."},{"q":"What is the drawback of using limit orders against slippage?","o":["It has none, it always offers a perfect solution","It may not fill if the price moves past the limit","It always reduces the execution speed provided by the broker","It always automatically incurs a higher commission"],"e":"The drawback of a limit order is that it does not fill if the price does not reach the specified level."},{"q":"What can you do to reduce slippage during news?","o":["Always use market orders, never limit orders","Use a reduced position size and limit orders","Always significantly increase the position size before the news","Specifically drop the stop-loss for the duration of the news"],"e":"During news, a reduced position size and limit orders help."},{"q":"Why does the reliability of the execution platform matter?","o":["An unreliable platform can itself increase slippage","It does not, every platform is always equally reliable","Because the platform never affects execution quality","Because only the broker's commission matters, not the platform"],"e":"An unreliable, slow platform can itself increase the actual amount of slippage."},{"q":"When is a market order worth using despite the slippage risk?","o":["When the exact price matters more than fast execution","Only at weekends, when there is no live trading anyway","Never, you must always use limit orders without exception","When fast, guaranteed execution matters more than the exact price"],"e":"A market order is worth using when fast, guaranteed execution matters more than the exact price."}]},
"97": {"t":"A Trading Plan Template","s":"What your written rulebook should look like","cards":[{"t":"Why a concrete template, not just a plan in principle?","b":"<p>In an earlier lesson you learned why you need a written plan — now let's look at <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">exactly what it should contain</strong> so that it is actually usable while trading, rather than remaining a theoretical document.</p>"},{"t":"The mandatory elements of the plan","b":"<p>A usable plan includes: <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">which assets you trade, at what time of day, the setup criteria for entering, where the stop/take-profit is, the position size, and the daily loss limit</strong>.</p>"},{"t":"Why use it as a checklist?","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">concrete checklist you tick off</strong> before every entry is far more effective than a general document of principles — it forces you to really think through the criteria instead of entering \"by feel\".</p>"},{"t":"Why review the plan from time to time?","b":"<p>Your plan is <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">not set in stone</strong> — it is worth reviewing and refining it objectively, based on your trading journal data across many trades, but never under the emotional impact of a single bad day.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"Which elements must a trading plan include?","o":["Entry criteria, risk management and exit rules","The company's full annual financial report in detail","Only the daily profit target, nothing else is needed","Only the broker's contact details and opening hours"],"e":"A good plan includes entry criteria, risk management rules and exit conditions too."},{"q":"Why is it worth building daily routine steps into the plan?","o":["It helps establish consistent, disciplined daily operation","Because the broker requires the routine to be officially recorded in writing","It is not, the plan should only cover trading rules","Because the daily routine never affects the quality of trading decisions"],"e":"Building in daily routine steps (pre-market, review) helps establish consistent, disciplined operation."},{"q":"Why is regular review of the plan important?","o":["It is not, once written the plan must stay unchanged forever","As the market and your own experience change, the plan can be refined","Because the law requires a mandatory monthly plan update","Because a review always improves the end result automatically"],"e":"As market conditions and your own experience change, the plan is worth refining and reviewing from time to time."},{"q":"Why shouldn't you change the plan emotionally, right after a bad day?","o":["Your emotional state can distort an objective assessment","Because a bad day never provides useful information about the plan","Go ahead, it is always worth reacting immediately to a bad day","Because changing the plan always improves the next day's result"],"e":"A decision made in an emotional state after a bad day can distort an objective assessment of the plan's real quality."},{"q":"What is the ultimate goal of a trading plan?","o":["Automatically maximising the daily profit, guaranteed in every case","Automatically reducing the broker's commission precisely every day","Providing a consistent, disciplined framework for decisions","Exclusively determining the market's official opening time"],"e":"The ultimate goal of a trading plan is to provide a consistent, disciplined framework for daily decisions."}]},
"102": {"t":"Platform Hotkeys","s":"The seconds that count at decisive moments","cards":[{"t":"Why do hotkeys matter in day trading?","b":"<p>In a fast market move, <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">clicking around with the mouse can cost seconds</strong> — a well-configured hotkey system (e.g. one key to close a position immediately) can reduce this delay to zero.</p>"},{"t":"Which functions are worth assigning hotkeys to?","b":"<p>The most common, critical functions: <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">instant market buy/sell, close all positions, adjust stop-loss</strong> — these are the actions where speed really matters.</p>"},{"t":"Why practise hotkeys on a demo account?","b":"<p>In a stressful live market situation <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">you should not be trying</strong> a new hotkey for the first time — practising on a demo account helps the movement become automatic by the time you need it live.</p>"},{"t":"Why can execution that is too easy and fast also be risky?","b":"<p>Paradoxically, <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">extremely fast, one-key execution</strong> can also make impulsive, thoughtless trading easier — you have to find the balance between speed and discipline.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"Why are hotkeys useful in day trading?","o":["Because the law requires every broker to offer hotkeys everywhere","They do not matter, clicking with the mouse is always just as fast","Because hotkeys automatically improve trade quality too","They can significantly speed up placing orders at critical moments"],"e":"Hotkeys can significantly speed up placing orders, which can matter at critical moments."},{"q":"Why is it risky to use hotkeys without proper practice?","o":["Because the broker automatically cancels faulty hotkey orders","Because hotkeys can never cause any error in practice","An accidental keystroke can place an unintended, wrong order","It is not risky, they can be used safely right away without practice"],"e":"An accidental keystroke can place a wrong order if there is not enough practice and care."},{"q":"Why is it worth practising hotkeys on a demo account?","o":["Because the broker only allows hotkey setup on demo","The correct, confident reflex can be built risk-free before real use","Because hotkeys work differently on demo than live","It is not, you should start using them live straight away"],"e":"On a demo account the reflex for using hotkeys correctly and confidently can be built risk-free."},{"q":"Which functions should you primarily assign hotkeys to?","o":["Frequent actions, such as closing a position or an order of a set size","Only calling the broker's customer service","Only changing the chart colour, no other function","Only fetching the daily report, nothing else is needed"],"e":"It is worth assigning hotkeys to frequent actions (closing a position, an order of a set size)."},{"q":"Why should the hotkey layout be customised?","o":["Fitted to your own familiar workflow, it reduces the chance of mistakes","Because customisation never affects the quality of trading in any case","It does not matter, the same layout is best for everyone","Because the broker always prescribes a uniform layout for everyone"],"e":"A customised layout fitted to your own workflow can reduce the chance of accidental mistakes while trading."}]},
"103": {"t":"The Psychological Difference Between Demo and Live Accounts","s":"Why you trade differently when real money is at stake","cards":[{"t":"Why doesn't demo success guarantee live success?","b":"<p>Many perform excellently on a demo account, then <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">their discipline completely collapses live</strong> — the risk of real money carries a psychologically very different emotional load than a virtual balance.</p>"},{"t":"What is the difference a demo cannot reproduce?","b":"<p>On a demo account <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">there is no real pain of loss</strong> (see loss aversion) — meaning demo performance can overestimate how you will behave with real money under stress.</p>"},{"t":"Why is a demo account still useful?","b":"<p>A demo account is <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">excellent for practising technical skills</strong> (chart reading, order types, using the platform) — only transferring the psychological side to live trading needs separate attention.</p>"},{"t":"How can you bridge this gap?","b":"<p>Many recommend that when you switch to live, you <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">start with extremely small stakes</strong> — this helps you gradually get used to the emotional load of real money before risking larger amounts.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"Why are demo and live trading emotionally different?","o":["Because a demo account always shows more accurate prices than live","There is no real difference, both give exactly the same experience","The risk of losing real money triggers a different emotional reaction","Because a live account automatically improves decision quality"],"e":"The risk of losing real money can trigger a completely different emotional reaction than the risk-free environment of a demo account."},{"q":"Why can someone be successful on demo but lose money live?","o":["Such a difference is not possible, both always produce the same results","Without the emotional pressure of demo, decisions can be made differently","Because a demo account always shows worse, distorted data","Because a live account automatically worsens the strategy's performance"],"e":"Because the emotional pressure (fear, greed) missing on demo is present with real money, different decisions can be made with the same strategy."},{"q":"Why is a gradual transition from demo to live recommended?","o":["You can gradually get used to the emotional load of real money","Because a gradual transition always slows learning unnecessarily","Because the broker requires a gradual transition from every client","It is not, you should always start with full stakes right away"],"e":"A gradual transition (starting with small stakes) helps you gradually get used to the emotional load of real money."},{"q":"What concrete signs reveal the extra stress caused by a live account?","o":["Hesitation, deviating from the plan or unusual agitation when deciding","Only the broker can tell, the trader never can","It cannot be recognised from any concrete sign, it stays invisible","It can only be determined afterwards from the monthly profit/loss"],"e":"Hesitation, deviating from the plan or unusual agitation when deciding can all signal the extra stress of a live account."}]},
"113": {"t":"The Pip in Forex Trading","s":"The smallest unit of movement in the currency market","cards":[{"t":"What is a pip?","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">pip (Percentage in Point)</strong> is the smallest traditional unit of price movement in the forex market — for most currency pairs it is the fourth decimal place (0.0001).</p>"},{"t":"The exception: yen pairs","b":"<p>For currency pairs that include the Japanese yen (e.g. USD/JPY) the pip is at the <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">second decimal place</strong> (0.01), because the yen's exchange rate is of a different order of magnitude than most other currencies.</p>"},{"t":"What is a pipette?","b":"<p>Many modern platforms already work with <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">tenth-of-a-pip (pipette) precision</strong> — this means the fifth decimal place for most pairs, allowing finer pricing.</p>"},{"t":"Why is it important to know the value of a pip?","b":"<p>The <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">dollar value of a pip changes with position size and currency pair</strong> — without it you cannot calculate exactly how much you gain or lose on a given price move.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What is a pip in forex trading?","o":["The name for daily trading profit","A type of stop-loss setting","The smallest traditional unit of price movement","A dividend paid by the broker"],"e":"A pip is the smallest traditional unit of price movement in the forex market, typically at the fourth decimal place."},{"q":"Where is the pip typically found for most currency pairs?","o":["Always at the first decimal place","In the whole-number part","At the fourth decimal place (0.0001)","At the second decimal place"],"e":"For most currency pairs the pip is at the fourth decimal place (0.0001)."},{"q":"Why are yen pairs an exception?","o":["Because of the yen's different order of magnitude, the pip is at the second decimal place","There is no exception, the rule is the same everywhere","Because yen pairs never move","Because trading the yen is forbidden"],"e":"For yen pairs the pip is at the second decimal place, because the yen is of a different order of magnitude than other currencies."},{"q":"What is a pipette?","o":["A type of swap fee","The broker's daily closing report","The old, outdated name for a pip","A finer pricing unit with tenth-of-a-pip precision"],"e":"A pipette is a finer pricing unit with tenth-of-a-pip precision, used by many modern platforms."},{"q":"Why is it important to know the dollar value of a pip?","o":["It is only useful for aesthetic reasons","Without it you cannot calculate the profit/loss of a move accurately","Because it determines the market's trading hours","Because the broker asks for it at registration"],"e":"Knowing the dollar value of a pip is essential for calculating profit/loss accurately, as it depends on position size and the currency pair."}]},
"114": {"t":"Margin Account and Cash Account","s":"The two basic account types that determine what you can do","cards":[{"t":"What is a Cash Account?","b":"<p>With a <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Cash Account</strong> you can only trade with your own capital that you have actually deposited — no leverage, no buying on credit.</p>"},{"t":"What is a Margin Account?","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">Margin Account</strong> lets you trade with capital borrowed from the broker (leverage) — this allows larger positions, but comes with the margin risks learned earlier.</p>"},{"t":"Why is it important to know which account you have?","b":"<p>On a Cash Account <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">you cannot open a short position</strong> in most markets (because it requires borrowing) — if you want to day trade in both directions, you need a Margin Account.</p>"},{"t":"What extra rules can apply to a Margin Account?","b":"<p>Margin Accounts are often subject to <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">extra regulatory requirements</strong> (e.g. a minimum balance, PDT-like rules) — always check the exact conditions of your own broker and jurisdiction.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What characterises a Cash Account?","o":["You can only trade with your own capital that you have actually deposited","Only short positions can be opened on it, no other type","Unlimited leverage is available in every case, without risk","There is no such account type, it is a theoretical, non-existent construct"],"e":"With a Cash Account you can only trade with your own deposited capital, without leverage."},{"q":"What characterises a Margin Account?","o":["It allows trading with capital borrowed from the broker","You can only trade with your own capital, never with leverage","It is only available to institutions, never to individuals","This account type carries no risk of any kind"],"e":"A Margin Account allows leveraged trading, using capital borrowed from the broker."},{"q":"Why can you typically not open a short position on a Cash Account?","o":["Because the law forbids everyone from opening short positions","You can, there is no restriction in this respect","Because a Cash Account only applies to forex, not other assets","A short position requires borrowing, which needs a Margin Account"],"e":"Opening a short position requires borrowing, which is not possible on a Cash Account, only on a Margin Account."},{"q":"What extra rules can apply to a Margin Account?","o":["There are never extra rules, the same apply as for a Cash Account","They only regulate chart colours, no other function","They only restrict the daily trading hours, nothing else","Extra regulatory requirements, e.g. a minimum balance"],"e":"Margin Accounts are often subject to extra regulatory requirements, such as a minimum balance or special rules."},{"q":"Why is it important to know your account type for day trading?","o":["A Cash Account can restrict opening short positions, which can matter","It does not matter, whichever type is available is fine","Because the account type never affects your options in any case","Because only a Cash Account allows day trading"],"e":"It is important to know the account type, because a Cash Account can restrict opening short positions, which matters for day trading strategies."}]},
"125": {"t":"Regulated and Unregulated Brokers","s":"Why it matters where your platform is registered","cards":[{"t":"What does a regulated broker mean?","b":"<p>A <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">regulated broker</strong> operates with the licence and under the supervision of an official financial authority (e.g. a national financial supervisor in the EU), meeting set capital and operational requirements.</p>"},{"t":"Why does this matter to you as a trader?","b":"<p>Regulation provides <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">a certain level of protection</strong> — e.g. it requires client money to be kept separate from the broker's own capital, reducing the risk of losing your money if the broker goes bankrupt.</p>"},{"t":"What risk does an unregulated (or weakly regulated) platform carry?","b":"<p>With a <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">platform that is not properly regulated</strong> there is no guarantee that client money is segregated, that execution is fair, or even that you can withdraw your money at all in a dispute.</p>"},{"t":"How can you check a broker's regulatory status?","b":"<p>Most regulators <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">keep a public register</strong> of licensed brokers — it is always worth checking that a platform actually appears in the cited regulator's register before transferring money to it.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What characterises a regulated broker?","o":["Only the oldest, longest-running platforms are regulated","It operates with the licence and under the supervision of an official financial authority","Any platform automatically counts as regulated","Only brokers that are completely free in all their services"],"e":"A regulated broker operates with the licence and under the supervision of an official financial authority."},{"q":"Why is regulation important for the trader?","o":["Because regulation guarantees a profit in every single case","It does not matter whether the broker is regulated or not","It provides some protection, e.g. segregated handling of client money","Because you can only lose money with regulated brokers"],"e":"Regulation provides a certain level of protection, such as keeping client money separate from the broker's own capital."},{"q":"What risk does a platform that is not properly regulated carry?","o":["It always offers lower fees, with no risk in every case","It only means a technical disadvantage, no financial risk at all","It has no extra risk, it is just as safe","There is no guarantee of segregated client money or fair execution"],"e":"With a platform that is not properly regulated there is no guarantee of segregated client money or fair execution."},{"q":"How can you check a broker's regulatory status?","o":["Only from social media opinions, other sources would be unreliable","It cannot be checked, you just have to trust the broker completely","In the regulator's public register","Only from the information on the broker's own website, never verified"],"e":"Most regulators keep a public register where a platform's licensed status can be checked."},{"q":"What should you do before transferring money to a new platform?","o":["Rely only on friends' recommendations, without checking","Rely only on the platform's look, other criteria would be unnecessary","Transfer immediately, without checking, every time without hesitation","Check the broker's regulatory status in the official register"],"e":"Before transferring money to a platform, it is worth checking its regulatory status in the official register."}]},
"126": {"t":"Client Money Protection and Insurance","s":"What happens to your money if the broker goes bankrupt","cards":[{"t":"What is the principle of a segregated account?","b":"<p>Regulated brokers are typically required to keep <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">client money in segregated accounts</strong>, separate from the broker's own operating capital — meaning that if the broker goes bankrupt, client money should in principle remain protected.</p>"},{"t":"What compensation schemes exist?","b":"<p>Many jurisdictions have some kind of <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">investor compensation scheme</strong> (in the EU this can be a different institution in each country), which compensates clients up to a certain amount if the broker goes bankrupt.</p>"},{"t":"Why doesn't this mean full protection?","b":"<p>These protection schemes typically <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">have a limit</strong>, and they do NOT protect against market losses — they only provide partial insurance against losing money due to the broker's bankruptcy/fraud.</p>"},{"t":"Why does this matter for diversification too?","b":"<p>If you trade with a significant amount, it is worth considering <strong style=\"color:#00D1FF\">not keeping all your capital with a single broker</strong> — this reduces your exposure if one platform runs into problems.</p>"}],"qs":[{"q":"What is the principle of a segregated account?","o":["There is no such principle anywhere, it is a theoretical, unreal construct","Client money is kept separate from the broker's own operating capital","Client money and the broker's own capital always sit in one shared account","Only large clients' money is segregated, smaller clients' never"],"e":"Under the segregated account principle, client money is kept separate from the broker's own capital."},{"q":"What do investor compensation schemes do?","o":["There is no such scheme anywhere in the world, it is entirely made up","They compensate clients up to a certain amount if the broker goes bankrupt","They only protect the broker's employees, never clients","They guarantee market profits in every case, without exception"],"e":"Investor compensation schemes compensate clients up to a certain amount if the broker goes bankrupt."},{"q":"What does this protection typically NOT cover?","o":["Market losses (e.g. if a position goes badly)","The broker's bankruptcy, which is exactly the main purpose of the protection","Broker fraud, which is also a basic purpose of the protection","It covers nothing, it is completely ineffective and pointless"],"e":"Investor compensation schemes do not protect against market losses, only against the broker's bankruptcy or fraud."},{"q":"Why do these protection schemes typically have a limit?","o":["Because it is only symbolic, formal regulation with no real purpose","Because they only protect very wealthy clients, no one else","They have no limit, they protect without limit in every case","Because of the scheme's sustainability and the spreading of risk"],"e":"Protection schemes typically have a limit, which serves the sustainability of the scheme."},{"q":"Why is it worth considering spreading capital across several brokers?","o":["It reduces exposure if one platform runs into problems","Because the law requires everyone to spread it, without exception","It is not, always keep all your money together with one broker","Because this always guarantees a higher return in every case"],"e":"Spreading capital across several brokers can reduce exposure if any one platform runs into problems."}]}
});
